          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"goethe-die-leiden-des-jungen-werther-0321a31ffd0dd044\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"The embedded clause “daß ich dich liebe” presents Werther’s love for Lotte as an actual emotional state, here characterized rhetorically as a worldly sin. It does not itself undertake a commitment. Although the letter is intensely emotional, the clause is syntactically and argumentatively integrated into a reflective avowal; there is no independent evidence that its production functions as a reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":null},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"written\",\"description\":\"Werther writes the love avowal to Lotte in his final letter; the novel reproduces that written communication within quotation marks.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"In a farewell letter written before his suicide, Werther defies the worldly significance of Lotte’s marriage to Albert and acknowledges his love and desire as a “sin” for which he will punish himself. His fervent claims that Lotte is his and that they will be united eternally reveal possessiveness, religiously framed fantasy, and extreme emotional agitation, but these contextual features do not change the highlighted clause’s core force from an avowal of love into an undertaking or reflexive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“für diese Welt Sünde, daß ich dich liebe” (“for this world [it would be] a sin that I love you”)\",\"supports\":\"The clause explicitly presents Werther’s loving state as true while evaluating it as sinful in the worldly order.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The avowal occurs within the rhetorical sequence “Und was ist das, daß Albert dein Mann ist? … Sünde?”\",\"supports\":\"Its grammatical and argumentative integration supports truth-conditional avowal rather than a reflex-like verbal outburst.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Nearby Werther writes, “Du bist von diesem Augenblicke mein! … Ich gehe voran!” and imagines eternal embraces after death.\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding letter is highly emotional and possessive, but its relational and eschatological claims are distinct from the core clause and do not independently give that clause performative force.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is marked as embedded, affirmative, present-tense, and subordinate in Goethe’s Die Leiden des jungen Werther.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the target is a subordinate explicit love predication rather than a standalone exclamation.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the highlighted clause as an avowal of a loving state. Its emotional, possessive, suicidal, and religious framing can be recorded contextually without adding P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
